From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 00:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Subtitles: translate a subtitle inside the video ahead of
 playback

A text subtitle inside the video (SubRip, plain text) reaches the
translation worker only as the player demuxes it, a few seconds before
each line is due. A model slower than the dialogue never builds a lead:
after an opening or a seek, every line is late. A picture subtitle
already had its track read a minute ahead in a demuxer of its own; a
text one did not.

The picture codec's read-ahead becomes CReadAhead, beside the cue
stage: the video opened again as playback opens it (a file through
CDVDInputStreamFile without the player's cache, so a network share is
read as playback reads it), the track found again by its id and codec,
and its packets handed to the codec's callback with the seek they were
read after.

Closing never waits for the network. CDVDInputStreamFile::Abort() only
sets a flag, and a read stalled on an NFS share returns when its
timeout runs out, while subtitles are opened and closed on the
player's thread. The read-ahead's state is shared with its thread,
which is let go: closing aborts the input, and waits only for a packet
being handed on, under the lock every callback - and every look at the
codec's match - takes; once closed, none is made, and the thread ends
by itself once its read returns.

After a seek the player flushes the subtitle before it moves its
clock, so the read-ahead starts from where playback goes: the player's
time once it has moved off where it was before the seek (two seconds
at most, for a seek to where playback already is), five seconds before
it. A read that fails before the end of the file is tried again from
where the reading was, three times, then the read-ahead stops and says
so in the log; the player's own packets go on as before.

Which cue came first, from which path, is CAheadMatch, shared too: a
cue is handed on once whichever path reads it first - matched by its
start (to 10 ms) and its text, so two lines starting together at
different places stay two - and one the read-ahead read before the
last seek is dropped. When the player reads a cue more than two
seconds behind where the read-ahead already is, the read-ahead does
not see the track as the player does, and is stopped.

A picture track is read as before: the whole film, a minute ahead of
playback, at up to four times its pace. A text subtitle of the video's
own streams (not one from a file beside it), opened to be translated,
in a Matroska or MP4 file, is read only to catch up: from each opening
or seek, the minute of the film after where playback goes, at twice
playback's pace with no head start, then nothing more until the next
seek - reading the file a second time for the whole film would double
what playback takes from the network. A transport stream is left out:
the player can shift its time stamps to keep them continuous, and a
cue read ahead would then not match the player's and show twice. A
disc, read through its own navigation, is left out too, and the repair
alone never reads ahead (CCueStage::Translates()).

The cues read ahead reach the cue stage early - repaired as before,
then queued for the worker - so their translations are ready before
the player's demuxer gets there; the player's own packet of a cue
already handed in is dropped, and the translation done, or under way,
is the one shown. A cue the read-ahead has not reached goes through as
before. The cues are handed in under one lock, so the repair and the
worker get them one at a time from either thread, and a seek waits for
the one in hand.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>

